# Speed Mode -- {{PROJECT_NAME}}

**Status:** Optional. Binding only once this project adopts it (see
SETUP-GUIDE.md, "Adding speed mode"). Read `.github/AGENT_RULES.md`
first: its tiers, git rules and Definition of Done win any conflict with
this file. Where this file adds a guardrail, the guardrail is binding;
where it describes a way of working, the project may adjust it.

## 1. What speed mode is, and is not

Speed mode is a way of running several AI agents in parallel without
lowering rigor. Speed comes from **parallelism and planning** -- disjoint
work running at the same time, with the next task always specified before
the last one finishes. It never comes from skipping a required review, a
live check, or a guardrail in section 2.

- **Opt-in.** A project, or a single session, turns it on on purpose.
  Without it, work follows `AGENT_RULES.md` alone.
- **It never lowers the tiers.** Tier policy is unchanged. A PR that
  needs a fresh adversarial review and a human merge under the tiers needs
  exactly that in speed mode too.
- **It cuts ceremony, not checks:** duplicated prose, ad-hoc test
  scaffolding, serial waiting, and over-provisioned models on low-risk
  changes.

## 2. Non-negotiable guardrails (never lowered, in any profile)

1. Anything that enforces **access, identity, secrets, CI or governance**
   (auth and permission rules, backend configuration, `.gitignore` and env
   handling, workflows, hooks, the rules files) gets a **fresh,
   independent review with real verification** -- run against the real
   system or a real throwaway account where one exists, not just a read of
   the diff. If the project's tiers make it Tier 2, a **human merges**
   it. Speed mode never touches "human merges".
2. Any **irreversible change** (a schema or data migration, a live
   configuration change, a deletion, a release) is reviewed **statically,
   as a draft, before it is applied** -- see section 7.
3. **Reviewer freshness and independence.** The reviewer starts with no
   shared context, never sees the author's rationale (only the diff and the
   claim), and must try to *add* cases the author's own tests lack. A
   reviewer that only re-runs the author's checks is a rerun, not a
   review. The author never grades their own tests alone.
4. **Verify the base before branching.** Fetch, then branch from the
   fetched default branch; check the base again before pushing. A
   background agent once branched from a stale local copy.
5. **Tier is decided by paths touched, not by judgment.** The lead is the
   one being reviewed, so the lead does not get to label the PR. If one PR
   spans classes, the highest class governs the whole PR.
6. **No secrets in prompts, logs, commit messages or checkpoints.** Agents
   read secrets from local environment files; nothing carries them.

## 3. Risk classes (path-based; the adopter fills this in)

Fill the path column from this project's own Tier 2 list and its real
layout. A change falls in the highest class any touched path belongs to.

| Class | Paths and changes | Profile |
|---|---|---|
| **Full** | {{FULL_RISK_PATHS}} -- at minimum everything in guardrail 1 | careful, never lowered |
| **Standard** | {{STANDARD_RISK_PATHS}} (for example server-side code and new queries or routes that are not Full) | standard |
| **Light** | {{LIGHT_RISK_PATHS}} (for example presentational code, copy, styles, docs with no new data access or auth logic) | fast allowed |

A change that looks like "just UI" but adds a data column, a permission,
or a route's auth logic is **not** Light. A new column on an existing
table can be silently covered by an existing broad rule; treat it as Full.

## 4. Profiles

- **careful** (Full): fresh adversarial review with real verification, a
  full re-run of the project's check suite, and a human merge where the
  tiers require one. A fix gets re-reviewed, unless it is narrow, covered
  by the existing checks, and verified against the real system -- then the
  check re-run plus a read of the prose is enough.
- **standard** (Standard): one fresh independent review plus green
  automated gates (types, lint, build, tests).
- **fast** (Light only): green gates plus the author's self-check; a
  reviewer is added only if a gate is red or the diff turns out to touch
  something above Light.

A profile is a floor. Raising it is always allowed; lowering below the
class's floor is not.

## 5. Roles and model classes

Use **model classes**, not hardcoded names: strongest, mid-tier, cheapest.
`MODEL-UTILIZATION-PLAYBOOK.md` says which concrete model fills each class
in this project.

| Role | Model class | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| Lead / integrator | mid-tier (session default) | Owns the lane plan, the shared files (section 6), merges, and the log. |
| Builder (parallel) | mid-tier | Own worktree, own branch, disjoint files only. |
| Security / adversarial reviewer (Full class) | strongest | Fresh context; runs real verification; must add cases beyond the author's. |
| Code reviewer (Standard / Light) | mid-tier, fresh | Gates must already be green. |
| Mechanical checks | scripts first; cheapest model only to triage | Never a judgment call. |
| Prose writer (log, PR body) | cheapest, with a word cap | A reviewer verifies any accuracy-critical pointer. |
| Planner / scout | cheapest, read-only | See section 6. |

**Cap concurrent strongest-class agents** (for example 1 to 2 at a time).
Usage limits are real: a strongest-class reviewer was once cut off
mid-run and left test data behind. The strongest class leads design and
the Full-class reviews; do not spend it on mechanical work.

Example mapping, as of 2026-10 (illustrative only; this Kit never
hardcodes model names, and these will go stale): strongest = a top-tier
frontier model, mid-tier = the session default model, cheapest = the
small fast model.

## 6. Plan before parallel; a planner stays ahead

No parallel work starts without a written **lane plan**: one row per task,
with files that do not overlap between parallel rows.

| ID | Task | Role / model class | Files it may touch (disjoint) | Risk class | Review type | Needs a human? |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| {{LANE_ID}} | {{TASK}} | {{ROLE_AND_CLASS}} | {{FILE_SET}} | {{CLASS}} | {{REVIEW}} | {{HUMAN_ASK_OR_NO}} |

Also write the dependency order (a task on the same files as another
waits for it) and the batch of human asks, ordered so the human can act in
one sitting.

A **planner / scout** (cheapest class, read-only) always stays ahead of
the build. It keeps the next lane's spec, one recommended default per open
decision, and the list of human asks ready, so the build never stops to
plan. The planner proposes; the lead decides.

## 7. Parallelism rules

1. **One writer per file set.** Shared files -- migrations, generated
   types, navigation or layout, shared design tokens, the rules files, and
   the progress or collaborator log -- are **lead-owned**; builders do not
   touch them.
2. **Every agent works in its own worktree** on its own branch. A shared
   checkout gets branch-switched under parallel agents.
3. **Branch from the fetched base** (guardrail 4).
4. **Serialize anything that mutates a shared live system** (a live
   database, a deployed service). Parallel agents use per-agent tags for
   test data and clean up by tag.
5. **Reviewers never see the author's rationale** (guardrail 3).
6. **Static review of a drafted irreversible change before applying it.**
   Have a fresh reviewer read the draft (the migration, the config diff)
   before any live apply. It is the cheapest high-value step: it caught
   holes before anything was applied. A change already applied live is
   committed in the same step; a live system ahead of the default branch is
   the main hazard if a session dies mid-PR.
7. **No secrets in prompts** (guardrail 6).

## 8. Save as you go; resuming

- Every builder commits a `wip: <what>` snapshot after **every logical
  step** and pushes **its own branch** (never the default branch, never a
  merge, never a force-push) after each commit.
- The to-do and done record lives in commit messages and the final
  report. **The branch history is the progress record.**
- Keep a ledger (`AGENT-LEDGER.md`) of agents, branches and resume
  recipes. It never contains credentials.

Three resume cases:

- **An agent hit a usage limit.** After the reset, resume the **same
  agent by id** (its context is kept): re-run `git log --oneline
  origin/<base>..HEAD` and `git status`, then continue from the first
  unfinished item.
- **The whole session ended** (or a new session or account). Fetch,
  list worktrees and open PRs, read each roster branch's commits to see
  what is done, then launch a fresh builder per unfinished branch with
  "the commits on it are finished; do not redo them; continue from the
  first unfinished item".
- **A read-only agent was cut off** (reviewer, QA). It left no commits;
  re-run it with the same brief and check for review branches it may have
  pushed. Clean up any test data it left.

## 9. Session limits

At a usage warning: bring the in-flight work to a safe state (committed,
pushed, nothing half-applied), then **pause. Never skip a required review
to save budget.** Prefer scripts before agents. Resume the same agent by
id rather than spawning a blank one.

## 10. Brevity caps

Log entries: about 150 words (what, why, findings, a link to the PR).
Commit messages: a subject plus at most 5 lines. PR bodies: change,
findings, risk, how to merge. Detail belongs in the diff and in code
comments, not in prose copies. Record the log entry, the metrics row
(`SPEED-MODE-METRICS.md`) and the progress note as you go; never let the
record lag the work.

## 11. Known traps (seen in real use)

- **Deleting a stacked PR's base branch closes the PR.** Merge without
  deleting the branch, retarget the dependent PR first, then delete.
- **A rebase forces a force-push** an agent may not be allowed to do. Push
  the rebased work under a new branch name and open a new PR.
- **A shared checkout gets switched under parallel agents.** Worktrees for
  every agent; start each from one canonical path form, because a
  differently-cased working-directory path broke worktree isolation on
  Windows several times.
- **An owner pastes a secret into chat.** Rotate it at once, and prefer
  flows where the secret never transits chat: set it from standard input
  into the secret store, or have the lead call the API with a scoped
  token. Where a short-lived scoped token must be pasted, record it in the
  ledger (no value) and revoke it after use.
- **A killed test run leaves test data.** Add a startup sweep that removes
  stale tagged data.

## 12. Honest limits

- Speed mode is **discipline plus review, not a mechanical guarantee.**
  Nothing here stops an agent that ignores it.
- The evidence comes from **one project**: several dozen PRs over about a week,
  one subsystem, one team. That is a small sample for general claims.
- **What was recorded:** blocking findings found before merge, blocking
  findings that escaped, which reviews ran, and rough time and token cost.
  **What was not measured:** a controlled comparison of a mid-tier against
  a strongest-class reviewer on the same Full-class change, and the
  net saving against a careful-only baseline. Time-saved figures in the
  source notes are estimates.
- No blocking issue escaped to the default branch in that project. That is
  a reason to keep measuring (`SPEED-MODE-METRICS.md`), not a proof.
- A green check suite proves only what its author thought of. The value of
  an independent reviewer is the cases the author did not.
